# 12. Search, AI quality and real-estate data quality

## 12.1 Search quality

CORE-005 remains the only canonical search/matching owner.

Required quality coverage:

- Typed criteria correctness.
- Filter enforcement.
- Locality normalization.
- Currency and unit correctness.
- Geo-radius correctness.
- Availability freshness.
- Ranking relevance.
- Tenant-scoped results.
- Source provenance.
- Unsupported criteria reporting.
- Deterministic behavior for known criteria.

## 12.2 Retrieval quality

Every AI-generated factual claim about an inventory item must be grounded in a retrievable, authorized source.

Required checks:

- Source record exists.
- Source is accessible to the requesting tenant/actor.
- Source is current enough for the claim.
- Source version is traceable.
- Citation resolves to the original record/evidence.
- Conflicting or missing sources are disclosed.
- Unsupported facts are refused or qualified.

A model-generated citation string is not evidence unless it resolves to a real source record.

## 12.3 AI evaluation

Separate:

- Deterministic unit tests.
- Retrieval-grounding tests.
- Representative benchmark datasets.
- Human-rated answer quality.
- Safety and adversarial tests.
- Provider live tests.
- Production quality/latency/cost metrics.

Evaluation thresholds must be numeric, versioned and approved.

A synthetic 10/10 test result cannot alone establish production readiness.

## 12.4 Financial and listing truth

Search and AI must preserve:

- Asking price versus final negotiated price.
- Currency and FX timestamp.
- Estimate versus verified figure.
- Carpet versus built-up versus other area type.
- Listing status versus verified inventory availability.
- Builder claim versus independently verified claim.
- Commission estimate versus approved commission.
- Deal stage versus completed settlement.

Ambiguity must be returned to the caller, not silently normalized into a false fact.

